  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "The two roots of the equation \\[ \\begin{aligned} a(b-c)x^2+b(c-a)x\\\\ {}+c(a-b)&=0 \\end{aligned} \\] are \\(1\\) and:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{b(c-a)}{a(b-c)}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{a(b-c)}{c(a-b)}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{a(b-c)}{b(c-a)}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{c(a-b)}{a(b-c)}\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(\\dfrac{c(a-b)}{b(c-a)}\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "d",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1954-p25.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "By Vieta’s formulas, the product of the two roots is \\[ \\frac{c(a-b)}{a(b-c)}. \\] Since one root is \\(1,\\) the other root equals this product.\n\nThus, the correct answer is D.",
      "hints": [
        "Use the product of the roots rather than solving the quadratic",
        "The product is the constant coefficient divided by the leading coefficient"
      ],
